 ## Overview
-Two-level Nested Ray Marching is a volume rendering technique that performs front-to-back compositing by traversing a coarse mesh of tetrahedral clusters and then marching through the fine-level tetrahedra contained within each active cluster [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently visualizing sparse, multi-resolution Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) data by avoiding the memory blow-up of uniform grid resampling while maintaining native resolution [S1, unofficial]. Unlike its sibling [Coarse Mesh Traversal](3062-coarse-mesh-traversal.md) which may use different sampling strategies, this method explicitly uses a constant adjustable step size for the inner loop and relies on macrocell density statistics for early-out culling [S1, unofficial].
+Two-level Nested Ray Marching is a volume rendering technique that performs front-to-back compositing by traversing a coarse mesh of tetrahedral clusters and then marching through the fine-level tetrahedra contained within each active cluster [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of efficiently visualizing sparse, multi-resolution Adaptive Mesh Refinement (AMR) data by performing a two-level nested march across coarse mesh elements and their descendant refinement tree elements [S1, unofficial]. Unlike its sibling [Coarse Mesh Traversal](3062-coarse-mesh-traversal.md) which may use different sampling strategies, this method explicitly uses a constant adjustable step size for the inner loop and relies on macrocell density statistics for early-out culling [S1, unofficial].
 
